# The session's timing, loaded once at process setup.
#
# Protobuf text of `brenn_reachy.cogs.config_clk_proto.SessionParams`, which the
# compiler generates from the `SessionParams` schema in `config.clk`. Read as it
# is: the box binds it by path, and the casing converts it to the message the
# dial hands the cog.

# How long one transaction has to be answered: 200 ms, ten driver cycles. A
# transaction the driver takes up is answered on the cycle it takes it up, so
# this is slack by an order of magnitude and is sized for the loss the command
# channel will suffer when it is a socket.
aux_timeout_ns: 200000000

# Three re-issues before the transaction is handed to the sequencer as a
# silence. The same datagram under the same correlation number each time, so
# what this buys is delivery and never a second ask.
aux_retries: 3

# Five nominal periods -- 100 ms -- with no fresh sample before the bus is
# declared failed. The driver publishes one per 20 ms cycle whether or not it
# read anything, so five missing in a row is a driver that has stopped rather
# than a bus that is having trouble.
sample_stale_after: 5

# Two seconds for the first sample. A process comes up with its cogs in an order
# nothing here fixes, and the driver's first cycle waits on its own execution
# condition; the machine is limp and uncommanded throughout, so the grace costs
# nothing but the delay in noticing a driver that never started.
#
# What it has to cover is the skew between two process starts the supervisor
# orders in no particular way, plus the driver's own init -- opening its port and
# writing the minimum risk condition, nine verified writes -- plus its first
# cycle. The ceiling on that init is the driver's own figure and is stated once,
# as `reachy_driver::STARTUP_INIT_BUDGET_NS`, in the crate whose startup path
# spends it. Nothing in the driver waits for anything else before it publishes:
# there is no hold and no gate in front of that first cycle.
#
# That sum is checked: `cogs/motion_cog_test.rs` asserts this figure against a
# skew allowance, the driver's init budget and two grid periods, so a grace cut
# under the terms it has to cover, or a driver that grows what it does before
# its first cycle, fails at `make check`. The driver-side terms are figures the
# tree states; the skew allowance is a budget, wider than any spread the
# 2026-08-29 launcher consoles show, and `docs/bench-runbook.md` records those.
#
# Only the first sample is budgeted here. Once samples are flowing,
# `sample_stale_after` above is what declares a driver that died, and it is
# untouched by any of this: a driver that stops mid-run is noticed as fast as it
# ever was.
startup_grace_ns: 2000000000

# Four seconds for a wind-down's one clock. Long enough for the configured stow
# move plus the settle it needs at the end of it, and short enough that a stow
# the machine is not completing is a conclusion rather than a wait.
stow_budget_ns: 4000000000

# Half a second for a commanded torque-off to be confirmed. The driver reads one
# row back per cycle, so a nine-row pass costs 180 ms; past this budget the
# session says so and keeps commanding, because nothing gates de-torquing.
torque_off_confirm_budget_ns: 500000000

# The servo-side motion profile the commission sequencer writes, in the
# registers' own units (0.229 rev/min for velocity, 214.577 rev/min² for
# acceleration). This pair has run on hardware but nobody has measured whether
# it is the right pair.
#
# The bench's trial-validated figures (400/600, including an 855°/s antenna sweep
# and the 0.8 s presence gesture) were sized for a host commanding whole moves,
# where the profile is
# only the backstop under host-side shaping and a tight one throttles the shaped
# motion itself. The session streams step-bounded setpoints instead, one per
# cycle, which is what the low pair is for.
profile_acceleration: 20
profile_velocity: 50

# The servos' own Bus Watchdog timeout, in the register's 20 ms units: ten of
# them, 200 ms. A servo whose bus has been silent that long stops -- and keeps
# holding torque, observed on this hardware. Armed anyway, because a stopped
# servo beats one chasing a stale goal once its driver is killed, crashed or
# unplugged mid-gesture; what answers the torque half of that state is nothing
# here. TODO(watchdog-holds-torque)
#
# Ten consecutive lost cycles is far past the measured jitter of a loop that
# writes a goal and reads a position every 20 ms while anything is held. The
# same 200 ms as the driver's own `hold_timeout_ns`, which is the same judgment
# about the same silence made one layer up.
bus_watchdog: 10

# Ten minutes of schedule horizon, the furthest ahead a script may put anything.
# A conversational sender holds the head up by refreshing its schedule on a
# cadence far under this; what the ceiling bounds is the sender that stops
# refreshing, whose last schedule is then the whole of the machine's committed
# future. Past its end the session stows and de-torques on its normal path, so
# this is the longest a dead sender can leave the machine engaged -- measured
# from the wake that took the script as well as from the script's own stamp,
# because a sender whose clock runs ahead would otherwise stretch that bound by
# its skew.
#
# Must be at least the longest schedule horizon a publisher can construct, as
# measured on this machine's clock at the wake that takes the script. The
# publisher's own ceiling is this same ten minutes, so the two numbers being
# equal leaves no room for a stamp from a clock running ahead: the invariant
# holds exactly when a script's arrival stamp is this machine's receive time.
# Any publisher here must stamp arrival with this machine's clock, never
# forward the sender's. TODO(reachy-pod-motion-integration)
script_span_cap_ms: 600000
